You did a great job testing that! You actually found a very specific piece of business logic that we need to address.
The system behaved exactly as it is currently coded. The reason it did not retry is because of how your code handles Manual Execution vs. Scheduled Execution.
If you look at your handleAsyncApprovalError method, you have this exact code:
        // 1. Check if this batch is handled by the Auto-Scheduler
        String scheduleFlag = "NO";
        // ... fetches from database ...

        // 2. Route the Status correctly with Exponential Backoff
        if ("YES".equalsIgnoreCase(scheduleFlag)) {
             // ... DO THE 5 RETRIES ...
        } else {
             // Immediate manual execution logic remains the same
             jdbcTemplate.update("UPDATE VOUCHER_POSTING SET BATCH_STATUS = 'P' WHERE BATCH_ID = ?", batchId);
             finalMsg = "Execution failed due to an unexpected database or server timeout. Please check server health.";
        }

Why it didn't retry:
When you created your test batch, you probably clicked "Approve Now" (Immediate Execution) instead of scheduling it for later.
Because it was an immediate execution, the database had IS_EXECUTION_SCHEDULED = 'NO'.
The code fell into the else block, pushed the status back to 'P' (Pending), and sent the final failure message without queueing it for a retry.
You are absolutely right!
You asked: "What if after some time it will be up, don't you think so?"
Yes! Even if a human clicks "Approve Now", if the database drops connection for 2 seconds, the system should be smart enough to wait 5 minutes and try again automatically instead of forcing the human to click it again.
How to fix it (Make EVERYTHING retry):
We need to remove that if-else restriction so that every failure (whether manual or scheduled) goes into the automatic retry queue.
Here is the exact replacement for that section inside your handleAsyncApprovalError method:
        // REPLACE THE ENTIRE "Route the Status correctly" SECTION WITH THIS:

        // A. Find out how many times it has already failed
        int retryCount = 0;
        try {
            retryCount = jdbcTemplate.queryForObject(
                    "SELECT RETRY_COUNT FROM SCHEDULED_BATCH_RETRY WHERE BATCH_ID = ?",
                    Integer.class, batchId
            );
        } catch (Exception ignored) {}

        // B. Exponential Backoff Logic
        int delayMinutes = 5;  // Default for Attempt 1
        if (retryCount == 2) delayMinutes = 10;
        else if (retryCount == 3) delayMinutes = 20;
        else if (retryCount >= 4) delayMinutes = 40;

        // C. Queue it for Retry! (Forces the Scheduler to pick it up even if it was originally manual)
        jdbcTemplate.update(
                "UPDATE VOUCHER_POSTING SET BATCH_STATUS = 'Q', IS_EXECUTION_SCHEDULED = 'YES', SCHEDULED_TIME = SYSTIMESTAMP + NUMTODSINTERVAL(?, 'MINUTE') WHERE BATCH_ID = ?",
                delayMinutes, batchId
        );

        String finalMsg = "Batch execution delayed due to network or database timeout. The system will auto-retry in " + delayMinutes + " minutes (Attempt " + Math.max(1, retryCount + 1) + " of 5). Please wait.";
        
        progressService.sendProgress(batchId, executorId, 0, "FAILED", finalMsg);

What this changes:
Now, if you intentionally write a bad SQL procedure, or if the database goes down, even an "Approve Now" click will automatically flip IS_EXECUTION_SCHEDULED = 'YES' and put the batch into the Q status. The background scheduler will now wake up 5 minutes later and try it again!
